menu "Pocket VM (L0 measurement probes)"

    config POCKET_VM_PROBE
        bool "Enable L0 VM probes (docs/quickjs-freertos-vm-spec.md sec.5)"
        default n
        help
            Off by default and meant to stay that way for every normal build.
            On, this compiles in:
              - main/pocket/vmprobe.c, which samples the guest's JS memory
                usage, free heap, largest free block and ui_task's C stack
                high-water mark every 8th frame (frame time and job counts
                every frame) and logs a rate-limited
                "VMPROBE ..." summary (min/median/max over a ~1s window)
                instead of one line per frame.
              - a #ifdef-guarded probe block inside the vendored
                components/quickjs-ng/quickjs-ng/quickjs.c that exposes
                sizeof(JSStackFrame)/sizeof(JSVarRef) (private to that file)
                and a job-queue depth counter around JS_EnqueueJob /
                JS_ExecutePendingJob.
            The six USB-only L0 workloads that used to live under apps/vmprobe/
            (letters A-F) were retired once L0 finished (tag vm-L0);
            `git checkout vm-L0 -- apps/vmprobe tools/vm_l0_capture.py`
            brings them back for a re-measurement. With this on, the probe
            above now samples whatever app is actually running.
            Build a separate build_* directory with this on -- and give that
            directory its OWN sdkconfig file (-D SDKCONFIG=<dir>/sdkconfig;
            see sdkconfig.vmprobe.defaults), because ESP-IDF's default
            sdkconfig path is the project root, shared by every build_*
            directory regardless of -B. Never turn this on in
            sdkconfig.defaults or in the shared root sdkconfig.

    config POCKET_VM_SCHED
        bool "Job-boundary scheduling and wake (docs/vm-L1-design.md, L1)"
        default y
        help
            The L1 execution control. On, a turn's Promise job drain may stop
            BETWEEN two jobs once the turn's time budget is spent, and the rest
            of that drain runs at the top of the next turn before any host
            event or frame() reaches JavaScript; a completion posted from
            another task can also cut short the frame-cap sleep instead of
            waiting out the whole period.

            Off, every knob it adds is set to "unlimited": the drain cannot
            yield, so no continuation turn can exist, the unhandled-rejection
            report point is the one it always was, and the frame cap is the old
            unconditional vTaskDelay. That is the pre-L1 behaviour exactly,
            which is what spec sec.12 means by every stage being revertible --
            if a board run finds something this level broke, this switch puts
            it back without reverting the code.

            Measure the cost with tools/memlog.py rather than assuming it.

endmenu
